Heinkel: [Although, it’s not like I’ve seen the patients for myself. Ultimately, the decision rests with the healers, but it’s more realistic than trying to figure out a way to get all the dragon carriages through, and if a route to the eastern ward can be secured while going through with the makeshift solution, everything should go smoothly from then on. ――Well, how’s that for now, Miss Filóre?]

Filóre: [Eh? Ehh! Yes! Ah, but, what about the people who can’t be moved from the treatment center…]

Heinkel: [It’s not like everyone in the treatment center is a severe case. Thus, as long as we pack a single crate with the most urgent medicines, it’ll last for the time being.]

Filóre: [I see! And as for that crate, I shall――]

Heinkel: [Oi…]

Filóre: [――Not do it, rather, I entrust it to you! After all, carrying things is your job!]

Pierced by Heinkel’s eyes, Filóre was discouraged, before turning a smile in the direction of the young worker. After he had gotten the catalog back from Heinkel, he nodded at Filóre’s words,

Worker: [Understood, I will act accordingly. As for the treatment centers…]

Filóre: [Please, rest assured. The treatment centers and the Church of the Divine Dragon are deeply involved with each other!  We will promptly send an envoy and act to ensure there are no misunderstandings. You may focus on your own duty.]

Worker: [――Yes! Both of you, thank you very much!]

Following an expression of relief, the young man bowed his head along with a sense of duty and left the chapel. After seeing him off, Heinkel slumped his shoulders in exhaustion.

