???: [――You’re a harder worker than I thought you’d be, you know?]

At the time of day when orange beams of the setting sun flowed in through the large clerestory, a voice called out from behind Heinkel while he was glaring at some documents in a room of the church.

When he turned around, the one staring at him from the entrance to the room was a man of slender figure and gentle features―― a clergyman of the Church of the Divine Dragon, and the one charged with acting as Filóre’s bodyguard, Tiga Rauleon. 

Before her participation in the Royal Selection, it seemed like he had accompanied Filóre as one of her attendants; upon Heinkel’s appointment as her Knight, the two of them met for the first time, and since then have run into each other quite frequently. 

As one would expect, Tiga’s initial impression towards Heinkel was the worst―― no, that was not a matter limited to just Tiga, the same could be said for all the people in Filóre’s vicinity. In reality, the only person who welcomed Heinkel’s appointment as her Knight, was Filóre herself.

Heinkel: [Well, that’s only natural. Anybody would react like that, hell, even me.]

Looking at it objectively, Heinkel’s current position was far beyond his measure.

Even at her best, Filóre was already reckless, and caused unceasing worry to those around her, so placing someone like Heinkel, with all the baggage and infamy that came with him, so close to her, was an act that no person with any level of judgment could possibly accept.

Regardless of Filóre’s own “Dragon’s Roar”, it would have likely been more difficult to find anyone who had actually been convinced of this than those who had not.

Tiga: [――? What’s with the sudden distant look? Did you not hear my snide remark?]

Heinkel: [No, I heard it alright, and I sympathize with you all. Even though it’s probably already hard enough being Miss Filóre’s attendant, being forced to take someone like me along too is, well…]

Tiga: [If you can understand that, I would’ve liked you to have politely turned her down to begin with.]

Heinkel: [Can’t argue with that. That would’ve been the proper answer for an adult, but…]

Cutting his words short there, Heinkel hesitated on whether to finish what he was about to say. But, pressed by Tiga’s gaze for the words that followed, he reluctantly opened his mouth.

The reason he had not refused the hand Filóre had offered him, was as plain as day.

Heinkel: [Well, it’s something like an ignoble obsession, a far cry from any mature judgement.]

If adults were people who could accurately discern where to call things off and end them, knowing when to give up, then Heinkel was a horribly wretched creature who had failed to fully grow into an adult. 

But, on the other hand, the adult whom Heinkel had admired more than anybody else in this world was a person who never let go of the sword until the very end of the end, cutting through even impossibility itself while never giving up on the things that he loved.

That was something he had yearned for, something he would never be able to reach, something that not even the tips of his fingers would ever be able to touch upon.

That might have been the genesis of Heinkel’s curse. ――Though even if he were to talk about this, Tiga likely would not be interested in the slightest.

Tiga: [――. I’ve done some digging into your situation. Don’t think ill of me. I don’t want anyone dangerous being by Filóre’s side. Besides, part of it’s already quite the well-known tale.]

However, at the tone imbued into Tiga’s following words, Heinkel raised an eyebrow.

He had lived a long time examining people’s expressions, so he knew the delicate nature that existed in the subtleties of negative emotions filling the likes of contempt, ridicule, pity, and sympathy. That was precisely why it made him want to incline his head.

After all, within Tiga’s voice, there had unmistakably been a hint of concern for Heinkel.

Tiga: [That thing you’ve been scowling at with a glum look for a while, is it Filóre’s itinerary? It’s quite difficult to get that girl to stick to her schedule, isn’t it?]

Paying no mind to his suspicions, Tiga stole a glance at the documents from behind, before making an appropriately insightful comment about Filóre.

In fact, tomorrow’s schedule―― with activities such as making courtesy rounds as a Royal Candidate, and fulfilling her duties as a Saintess of the Church, Filóre’s day to day had a dizzying busyness to it. Even with what was not written down on the itinerary, she would attend the divine service that coincided with sunrise in the morning, and after she finished her prayers, she would voluntarily lend an ear to the people who visit the church in search of counsel.

Heinkel: [And in the end, she’s the type who’ll absolutely never overlook any problem that presents itself to her. If she comes across a stray child by the roadside, or an elderly person facing difficulties carrying something, her plans for later will be doomed.]

Tiga: [Ah, I know what you mean. One time in the past, Filóre went missing, and it turned out that she had gone off to help an elderly person carry something, accompanying them three major streets away, before being treated to tea in their home and coming back.]

Heinkel: […As long as her title is Saintess, it should be fine. Though actually, the moment she gained the title Saintess, doing stuff like that was plenty reckless.]

Now that she had become a Royal Candidate, she could no longer do as she had been until that point.

When one gains an irreplaceable role, it comes with tasks that cannot be delegated. Such was expected of Filóre too, and it went without saying which thing had a greater degree of importance. 

It went without saying, but――,

Heinkel: [It’s not like Miss Filóre wants to neglect her duties as a member of the Church either, and on top of that, she also wants to devote herself to being a Royal Candidate… it’s all way too excessive.]

Being made to accompany that enthusiasm, Heinkel had to keep busily running about all over the place.

In place of Filóre, who was always poking her head into everything, he was utterly swamped with work that did not befit a first Knight in the slightest, pondering the best person to assign any given problem that emerges to, in order to best get it solved, rescheduling her delayed plans, making apologies to those who were scheduled to meet with her, and preparing in advance the documents and proposals that she would likely need next.

In fact, ever since he had become Filóre’s Knight, the opportunities for him to draw his sword had numbered zero.

Tiga: [Even so, while Filóre attends service every morning, it seems like you never skip out on practice, huh? Though, I’d made the assumption that you surely wouldn’t be a morning person.]

Heinkel: […You think someone who’s bad at waking up early would ever be fit as a Knight?]

Tiga: [I see, it’s seeming more and more like I’d be ill-suited to it. I’m glad the grand duty of being a Knight didn’t end up falling to me. Either way, I probably wouldn’t be able to deny my lack of strength.]

While adjusting the hat on his head, Tiga analyzed himself in a carefree manner, and Heinkel examined him through the corner of his eye.

Heinkel: [Lack of strength, huh.]

To be honest, Heinkel was unable to agree with Tiga’s comment just now.

To begin with, wearing a curved blade by his waist, Tiga was strong enough to be appointed as Filóre’s bodyguard, and even judging by Heinkel’s eyes, his strength was nothing insignificant. His physique was flexible and well-tempered, and the way he carried himself showed that he also had ample real combat experience; above all, he possessed an outstanding grit that would not be perturbed by anything.

He was neither a swordsman nor a Knight, however, he was a warrior. ――That was Heinkel’s appraisal of him.

Be that as it may――,

Heinkel: [So, how are your wounds?]

Tiga: [Hmmm, I guess I can’t say I’ve fully recovered yet. Still, they’ve gotten quite a bit better, but they were quite deep gashes to begin with… As expected, her alias of Valkyrie isn’t just for show.]

Heinkel: [The one who killed Sir McMahon and fled the scene, Duchess Karsten, huh…?]

Tiga gave his answer while patting the side of his torso through his clothes, causing Heinkel to furrow his brow.

The name he had voiced belonged to none other than the very person responsible for inflicting the deep wound upon Tiga’s flank, who was considered to have lit the fuse on the maelstrom of mayhem enveloping the Royal Capital at the moment. 

――Crusch Karsten, also known as the Valkyrie, who at first, had been the front runner to win the Royal Selection. And now, she was wanted for being the leading perpetrator behind the assassination of the central figure of the Council of Wise Men, Miklotov McMahon, her whereabouts under pursuit all throughout the Kingdom.

The incident itself had come as quite the shock, but what he found truly incomprehensible was the fact that Natsuki Subaru, who ought to have been Emilia’s Knight, had also been deemed a wanted fugitive for his complicity in Crusch’s actions.

Furthermore, complicity was not the only charge he was facing, as he had even been placed under the exceedingly unbelievable suspicion of being none other than the Sin Archbishop of Sloth.

Accompanying that, both Emilia, who was suspected of harboring rebellious intentions towards the Royal Castle, and Felt, who was unable to dispel the doubts regarding the falsification of her identity, had been taken into custody at the castle, and the members of both Camps fled the Royal Capital―― at that juncture, gargantuan Witchbeasts had been set loose in the town, and the Sword Saint had been mobilized to bring the situation under control.

All of that had already transpired before Heinkel was brought to the Royal Capital, and even now, those events continued to spread a nightmarish chaos throughout not only the Royal Capital, but throughout the entire Kingdom.

Heinkel: [By now, the stark atmosphere of the Royal Capital will be something that goes down in the Kingdom’s history. On the streets, the Knights and the guards all have an imposing air about them, and the townsfolk have nothing cheerful to talk about, so spend all their time just keeping their heads down.]

Tiga: [For the people who have a choice, leaving the Royal Capital is also possible. But, those who don’t are an overwhelming majority. To be honest, the Church is also the busiest it’s been in years.]

Heinkel: [And, that also strengthens the support for Miss Filóre, huh?]

Tiga: [I’m not fond of that way of thinking. Not just me, but Filóre too.]

As Heinkel threw in a cynical remark, Tiga sharpened his gaze upon him.

It seemed like that was not intended as a joke, and it was something that had seriously displeased him, so Heinkel raised both his hands to demonstrate his capitulation.

But, regardless of Tiga’s feelings, in practice, that was how things were progressing.

The more chaos spread in the Kingdom, the more people’s belief in the Royal Castle would be sprained, with their hearts instead beginning to take to the Church that worshipped the Divine Dragon, which had continued to safeguard the Kingdom with a proven track record.

As a result, that had unmistakably become a tailwind for Filóre, who had gotten a late start.

And, for Heinkel too, with his position as Filóre’s Knight, it was certainly something that ought to inspire deep emotion within him, but――,

Heinkel: [Honored Father…]

Across Heinkel’s mind as he muttered, there flashed the existence of Wilhelm, his biological father whose whereabouts were currently unknown.

Crusch was wanted by the authorities for the crime of Miklotov’s assassination, and Wilhelm, who had served her, was one of the relevant parties who had disappeared from the Royal Capital. It was conceivable that he had reconvened with his master, and was assisting her on the run, but it went without saying that they were at an overwhelming disadvantage.

Of course, if it was the famous Sword Demon, he might very well end up cutting through any adversity with that sword of his. Though it was not as though such dream-like expectations did not reside within Heinkel.

Tiga: [You also have it tough, don’t you, Heinkel-san.]

As though he had sensed Heinkel’s inner heart from his mutter and expression, Tiga shrugged.

Having it tough, that comment was probably right. But, if he were to bring that up, the entire Astrea Family had been fragmented ever since Heinkel had joined Priscilla’s camp.

Heinkel: […My own difficulties aren’t anything important. The only problem is that this might cause trouble for Miss Filóre. My honored father and my son… they’re both fully grown men. It’s only natural that everyone wipes their own ass, right?]

Tiga: [――. At the very least, your father was not present at the scene where I crossed blades with Duchess Karsten. It would be good if it turns out the suspicions against your father are unfounded.]

Tiga spoke those words and patted him on the back in encouragement, and after Heinkel hesitated on how to respond, he half-heartedly returned nothing but a crude “Ahh”.

The comment about Wilhelm not having been present there, it was something he could have figured out even if it had not been expressly stated. If Wilhelm had been there, and Tiga had ended up crossing swords with him, Tiga would no longer exist in this world today.

Besides, there was one other reason why his reply had been so vague. And, that was something he would absolutely never reveal to Tiga.

――After all, it was because within Heinkel, a seedling of indelible distrust towards the Church of the Divine Dragon had begun to sprout.

